        作品概要
      </h2>

      <p
        class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-base leading-10 text-[#55504a]"
        style="font-family:'Shippori Mincho',serif;"
      >
        Edo Castle, built in what is now Chiyoda, Tokyo, was one of the largest castles of early modern Japan and served as the political and military center from which the Tokugawa shogunate governed the country.<br>
        Throughout the Edo period, it supported the development of Edo, the largest castle town in Japan.<br>
        After the establishment of the Edo shogunate in 1603, Tokugawa Ieyasu led large-scale expansion and development, and the magnificent castle was completed through nationwide construction projects known as <em>tenka-bushin</em>.<br>
        With vast grounds and magnificent structures including a five-story Main Tower, Edo Castle became known as the greatest castle in Japanese history, both in name and in reality.<br>
        265 years after the construction projects of the Keicho period, Edo Castle became the Imperial Castle. Edo, the castle town surrounding it, was renamed Tokyo and became the capital of Japan, and continued its remarkable development for roughly another 150 years to the present day.
      </p>

    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto mt-20 max-w-6xl"></div>

    <!-- Information -->

